node_modules/

# ─── Secrets ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Both patterns are needed, and the reason is the bug this file used to have:
# `.env.*` matches `.env.local` and `.env.production` but NOT the bare `.env`,
# because the pattern requires the dot and something after it. So the one file
# that actually held JWT_SECRET and EMAIL_PASS was the one file not ignored —
# untracked, but a single `git add .` away from being public forever.
#
# Rule of thumb: ignore the exact filename first, then the variants.
#
# Verify with `git add --dry-run .` (which prints what WOULD be staged) or
# `git status --ignored=matching`. Do NOT rely on `git check-ignore -v` here: with a
# negation in play it reports the last matching rule in a way that is easy to
# misread as "this file is ignored" when it is not.
.env
.env.*

# ...but the committed template has to survive the rule above, or nobody can
# tell which variables the app needs. A negation must come AFTER the pattern
# it undoes; gitignore is last-match-wins, not first-match-wins.
!.env.example

# ─── Test / build output ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
coverage/
*.log
npm-debug.log*

# ─── OS noise ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# ─── Local-only notes ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Working notes that are useful on this machine but must never be published:
# local database ports, absolute paths containing a username, git identity
# reminders. The committed counterpart is CLAUDE.md, which is deliberately free of
# anything machine- or person-specific.
#
# The `*.local.*` shape is the convention worth keeping: it makes "this file is
# private" visible in the filename itself, so a future file does not need a new
# ignore rule and nobody has to remember one.
*.local.md
*.local.json